for development

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.593
and operations of environments.

00:01:06.593 --> 00:01:09.010
When it comes to benefits of
a Cloud Composer environment,

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:11.880
first of all, I would
cloud the deployment speed.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.320
You can get started
with the product very,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.680
very quickly and
practically in 20 minutes,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.300
get an environment
up and running

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.410
without having any prior
experience with Apache Airflow.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.510
And since this is a managed
environment, what you're also

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.230
gaining is the
fact that you don't

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:33.790
need to manage your own patches
or underlying components

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.600
and upgrade them as needed.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.210
And you also don't need to
look into reliability metrics

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.950
and essentially worry about
having your product affected

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.720
by outages of
underlying component.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.370
This is all taken care
of as a managed product.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.630
The cost of using Cloud
Composer is predictable.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.260
And I would even argue it
is well-optimized for use.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.720
What I mean by this
is that it optimizes

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.190
for a total cost of ownership.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:02.570
And, therefore,
the fact that you

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.210
don't need to manage individual
components of the platform

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.580
of the architecture
of the infrastructure,

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.380
you're saving time
that obviously

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.820
can be accounted for also
from a cost perspective.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.250
Since this is a product
fully compatible

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.740
with open-source Apache
Airflow, the code

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.380
that you run in
Cloud Composer is

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:23.660
fully portable to its
open source counterparts.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.420
And, therefore, you can
freely move your DAGs

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.790
into Cloud Composer
from Apache Airflow

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.820
and out obviously assuming we're
talking about the same versions

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.000
without a fear of being locked
into a specific platform

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.930
or specific product.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.180
Enterprise customers
will specifically

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.670
appreciate the fact that
Cloud Composer comes

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.370
with multiple
security features that

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:46.630
are available out of the box.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.172
And we're going to
cover some of them

00:02:48.172 --> 00:02:49.700
later on in today's
presentation.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:54.070
But elements, like private IP
or shared VPC or even network

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.290
perimeter are separated out
of the box by the environment.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:01.230
And you don't need to spend
time on configuring those.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.290
Last but not least, Cloud
Composer as a managed product

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.240
also comes with
technical support.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.010
So in case there was
any outage or issue

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.660
that you may need to
have some help with,

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.620
you can always refer to
Google Cloud Support asking

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.230
for additional assistance.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.090
Obviously that follows
your agreements

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.040
with the technical
support from Google Cloud

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.270
that you have in
your organization.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:26.850
So without further ado, let me
dive into development practices

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:30.600
that hopefully will maximize
the benefit and experience

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.100
from using Cloud Composer.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.820
Let me start with a somewhat
pessimistic but actually

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.630
optimistic message, which
is that every task can fail.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.510
And while it may sound like
not that encouraging, what

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.150
I mean by this is
that, in fact, you can

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.000
and you should account
for potential failures

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.240
and build some
measures into your DAGs

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.170
that's recover
from those issues.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:57.030
And a great example is using
a built-in retry policies

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.300
that can recover from issues,
for instance, network outages

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.750
and, therefore, get your DAG
properly executed, successfully

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.090
completed, even if a particular
task goes down because of,

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:08.640
for instance, a network issue.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.720
So always remember about
building a proper retry

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.000
policies directly from
the very beginning.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.470
And it shouldn't really
be an afterthought.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:21.550
It should be a part of our
regular development practices.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.630
However, for retrace
to work properly,

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:28.930
it is very important to
have your tasks important.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:32.430
What I mean by this is
ability to run a specific task

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.180
multiple times and expecting the
exact same result every time.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.330
Now, imagine that you
are creating some kind

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.080
of an entry in the database.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.890
And you insert into
table using a command.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.290
And then you have to
rerun the same task.

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:49.530
By doing so, you may end
up having multiple entries

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.230
simply duplicated in
the same database table.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.950
That's probably not
the best approach.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:57.510
And this task may not
be idempotent as such.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.720
And, therefore, a better way to
handle the exact same request

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.490
is to, for instance,
insert or replace

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:06.330
with a specific key
that is assigned

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:09.750
to the specific transaction
and, therefore, if repeated,

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.840
will land in exactly the same
place in the database and worst

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.430
case, will simply
overwrite the same entry.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.460
And this way, a
particular entry that

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.930
inserts the data
into the database

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.870
becomes idempotent
and works very well

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.270
with a potential
retry mechanism.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.730
So always remember to
use retry and think

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:28.770
about idempotency of
the tasks that you're

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.960
orchestrating with it.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.580
While developing Airflow
docks, it is very important

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.060
to leave the work that needs
to be implemented and executed

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:44.200
as part of your workflow
for operators and tasks.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.080
There is a temptation
to put some logic,

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.150
some transformations
outside of the tasks

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:50.530
and outside of operators.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:51.910
But the challenge
with doing that

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.510
is that Airflow doesn't really
then have a great visibility

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:57.970
into those activities
and also [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.640
delegate this work to
individual workers, which

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.430
is not ideal from end-to-end
management of your workflow

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.350
logic.

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:08.170
So the rule of thumb
should be to have your DAG

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:12.730
as a very simple and streamlined
piece of code that delegates

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.520
all of the work to individual
tasks that are implemented

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.830
using proper operators.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.830
Speaking of operators,
you will also likely

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:24.570
need, obviously, as
part of the Airflow DAGs

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.870
to interact with
many other products.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.120
And you may end
up in a case where

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.910
you realize that you have never
used a particular integration

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:32.710
before.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:35.510
And you want to start creating
your own operator or a sensor

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:39.590
or a hook to essentially connect
to those external systems.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.690
My hint on this
specific scenario

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.480
would be to always look into
whether there is already

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.580
an existing operator
or integration

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:51.770
created for that specific
interface and try to use that.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:54.830
The community has put tremendous
amount of work and effort

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:59.240
into building operators that
are available for the most

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.840
common interfaces, the most
common products, and also

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.070
protocols and this way enable
a broad usage of Airflow

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.470
with orchestration of
those different products

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:10.590
and services.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.640
So, again, instead of trying
to write their own operators,

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.400
always try to see if you can use
one of those that are already

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.700
there and benefit from what
the community has done.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.350
And if you need to
create another one,

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.650
and you realize that somebody
else may benefit from it,

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.400
you may also consider
contributing it back

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.330
to the community.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:30.940
While creating your
DAGs, you will often

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:35.230
get into cases where to properly
implement your business logic,

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:39.430
you need to be supported by
some modules that are delivered

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.520
outside of your specific DAG.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.640
And typically you would
rely on PyPi modules--

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:48.340
basically PyPi
modules that extended

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.770
the capability of your DAG.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.250
And the best way to manage
installation of the PyPi

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.750
modules is through a
built-in feature that

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.670
is available in Cloud Composer.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:00.040
So this feature
allows you to define

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.970
which packages are necessary
for your individual DAGs.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:07.630
And they are being installed in
the environment as a dependency

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:09.310
for your DAGs and
obviously makes

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:13.030
them available right away for
your DAGs in their execution.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.640
One more hint I would
like to pass in here

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:20.750
is to avoid using a specific
version if you don't have to.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.320
And leaving it blank means
your more future-proof.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.830
And you're not specifically
limiting yourself to a version

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:30.660
that you have created and
used at this particular point

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.160
in time.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.000
So, again, use the specific
version on if you really

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.389
have to for particular reasons.

00:08:37.853 --> 00:08:39.270
There could be
cases where you may

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.840
want to create a
DAG that consists,

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.360
in fact, of a hierarchy
of different DAGs

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.160
or is somewhat modular.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.620
And this could be to
abstract a piece of work

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.550
and keep it as a contained
piece of logic that's basically

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:55.770
a bigger DAG can interact with.

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.900
And there is
frequently a question

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.880
on how to best deal
with this scenario

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.130
should I use SubDAGs
or some other way.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.290
And I will personally not
recommend using SubDAGs.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:05.790
And the primary
reason is that there

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.325
could be some operational
issues with doing that--

00:09:08.325 --> 00:09:10.830
[INAUDIBLE] locks and
many other scenarios.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.080
And as such, probably
one of the best ways

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.890
to handle that specific case
is to use DAG Run Operator

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:17.660
instead.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.260
And this way from a
particular task of a DAG,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.140
make a request to other
DAGs and make them

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.120
executed as part
of your core DAG,

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.520
as we are sitting here
in this specific diagram.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:33.210
So, again, use DAG Run
Operator instead of trying

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:37.390
to use SubDAGs if necessary.

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:40.380
So these were the high-level
development practices

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.630
when it comes to coding of your
DAGs that I wanted to pass.

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:46.830
But there is also a lot of
hints and recommendations

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.510
I would like to
share when it comes

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:50.850
to architecting your
Composer environments

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:55.140
or managing them moving forward.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.037
So let me start with probably
one of the most important items

00:09:58.037 --> 00:09:59.870
that I would like to
call out in this space.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:03.380
And that is to upgrade your
environments frequently.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.900
The Airflow coming into is
putting tremendous amount

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.280
of work and effort in improving
the quality of the product

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:13.820
and shipping features that the
community will benefit from.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:17.240
Staying up to date with
the Airflow release

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.910
gives you access to all
of those new features

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:22.320
and bug fixes that the
community has created.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.950
And the rule of
thumb is to not fall

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:26.870
behind more than
six months from what

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:30.200
has been announced or
shared by the community,

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:33.680
and this way not falling
behind basically the benefits

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.110
to those new releases bring.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:38.120
Now, since the
upgrade process itself

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:41.450
can be somehow heavy
when it comes to effort,

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:44.270
there is a feature in Cloud
Composer that addresses

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.100
this challenge a little bit.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:47.600
And that is the
upgrade feature that

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.630
allows you to upgrade
in environment in place

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:53.690
and prevents you
or avoids the need

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.540
for creating a new environment
moving all of your DAGs

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.400
over or, for
instance, even worse,

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:01.160
losing the history
of your DAG runs.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.200
So, again, use the
upgrade feature

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:05.240
to keep your
environment up to date

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:08.120
and stay with the recent
version of Airflow

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.110
to benefit from all
the features it brings.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:13.910
I also want to spend
a moment talking

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:15.530
about security
features, because this

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:18.498
is important for
multiple customers.

00:11:18.498 --> 00:11:20.540
And obviously there is a
lot of security features

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:21.570
you can choose from.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.480
And I highly encourage
you to look into those

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:25.160
and pick the ones
that are best suited

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:28.040
for your organization and
your individual needs.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.440
The first one I would like
to call out is private IP.

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:33.500
With private IP called
Composer environments,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.880
the nodes that are being used
for compute of Cloud Composer

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:40.640
are not assigned
public IP addresses.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.730
So with that, they can't
really be addressed or accessed

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:46.080
from outside of the VPC network.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:49.550
And as such are protected
from a networking perspective

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:50.360
from such access.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.090
They still have access
to the outside world.

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:53.798
They can still reach
out to the internet.

00:11:53.798 --> 00:11:57.150
But they simply cannot be
accessed from the outside.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:02.210
Now, when it comes to the
web server of Cloud Composer

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:06.500
and user interface for
Airflow, the primary protection

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:10.040
mechanism and security mechanism
when accessing this component

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:11.400
is authentication.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.880
So only authenticated
customers and users

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:17.460
have access to a specific
Airflow user interface.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:20.600
However, some customers want to
have one more layer of security

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:22.080
for the web server as well.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:23.550
And that is a network layer.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.420
So if you want to have
network-level protection

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.370
for your Airflow
user interface, you

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:32.150
may want to use web server
access controls that's together

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:34.790
with private IP make
your environment not

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:39.200
accessible from the outside
world, unless the color belongs

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:43.890
to a predefined and whitelisted
set of IP addresses.

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:48.050
So this is like a firewall
setting that secures access

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:50.480
to your web server from
the outside world, which

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:52.820
you can use to configure
and further tighten access

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:55.490
to your Airflow web server.

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:58.660
Another feature that may
be used in conjunction

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:01.520
with other components
is VPC Native mode,

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.090
which puts your
Composer environment

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:05.560
and specifically
the GK classword

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:08.020
that it uses into
a VPC that allows

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:11.220
for access to other
components in the same VPC.

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:14.250
Customers that want
to have ultimate level

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:16.770
of network-level
protection may also

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.620
want to use a feature
called VPC Service Controls.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.140
What this feature
gives is ability

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.140
to configure a network
service parameter which

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:30.870
becomes a standalone isolated
network that prevents access

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.420
from the outside world
but also prevents access

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:36.090
from within the parameter
to the outside world

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:39.330
creating a totally separate
and isolated network.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:42.630
And services deployed into
such network perimeter

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:47.070
are ultimately secured from
network access perspective.

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:49.545
This setting, this
architecture also

00:13:49.545 --> 00:13:51.920
comes with its own challenges,
because, for instance, you

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.290
can't easily use
externally hosted PyPi

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.870
packages because this network
connection is not available

00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:58.690
anymore.

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:00.930
So while it comes with
additional complexities

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:04.030
to manage, it also comes
with increased security.

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:06.990
So some organizations may
choose to use this feature

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:10.520
to further secure their
Composer environments.

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:13.440
Two more features I would
like to call out for Composer

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.050
are shared VPC and
domain-restricted sharing.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:19.140
With shared VPC,
you can create a VPC

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:22.740
that becomes a central hub
that multiple products connect

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:26.520
to and exchange information
with one another through that

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:27.390
hub only.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.370
So this gives customers
a central place

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:33.230
of management for network
traffic and control.

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:36.540
And as such, if you create and
deploy Composer environment

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:39.250
to one of those projects,
participating in the shared VPC

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:43.590
setup, you gain a lot of
control over the network traffic

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:46.813
originated from this Composer
environment or coming into it.

00:14:46.813 --> 00:14:48.480
So this is something
that some customers

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:50.920
may want to consider as well.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:53.550
And the last security feature
I would like to call out

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.360
is the domain-restricted
sharing.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:57.210
And this feature
allows customers

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:00.240
to restrict specific
domain names that

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.970
need to be there
in the addresses

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:05.520
of individual
accounts for access

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:06.960
to the Composer environment.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:09.360
And as such, even if somebody
has been granted access

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:12.670
to your environment but does not
belong to a whitelisted domain,

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:15.390
which could be your
corporate domain name,

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:18.480
basically won't be able
to access your environment

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:21.870
without proper domain
name or email address.

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:24.540
So this is something
that customers can also

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.970
choose to further secure
their access to Composer

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.890
environments.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:33.310
Another feature that
might be worth considering

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:36.520
is integration with Google's
Secret Manager as a way

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.500
to manage secrets for your DAGs.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:41.890
It is very frequently the case
that when managing integrations

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:43.990
from Airflow DAGs
to other systems,

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:47.380
you need to use some
keys or some passwords

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.010
to connect to those
external systems.

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.385
And the question is, how do
you best manage those secrets

00:15:51.385 --> 00:15:53.950
and keys while doing so?

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:55.660
The recommended
approach from my side

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:58.450
would be to use Google's
Secret Manager as a back end

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:00.440
for managing such secrets.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:03.940
This integration has been
introduced in Airflow 1.10.10.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:06.420
So we need to use that
version on [? newer ?]

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:09.490
and has also been recently
added to a Cloud Composer

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:09.990
environment.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:11.490
And this is something
I would highly

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:13.150
recommend for all
the customers that

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:16.300
need to use secret keys,
passwords when integrating

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:18.070
with external systems.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:22.160
This recommendation or hint is
almost too obvious to mention,

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:25.893
which is to separate production
from nonproduction environment.

00:16:25.893 --> 00:16:27.310
And while it sounds
obvious, it is

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:31.300
important to call out that
as you create your production

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:35.470
pipelines that run your business
process and business logic,

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:37.810
you need to have a
separate environment

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:40.930
to test the newly created
DAGs or changes to those DAGs

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:41.940
separately from it.

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:43.315
And that's
particularly important

00:16:43.315 --> 00:16:46.150
because, not only you need to
have management separation,

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:50.110
but you also need to have, well,
full domain separation, as well

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:55.450
as management separation
and performance and capacity

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:56.320
separation.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:58.835
So for all those
reasons, you should

00:16:58.835 --> 00:17:01.210
have at least two environments--
one for production-- one

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:02.560
for other use cases.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:05.260
Obviously, you can end up
with multiple environments

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:07.089
for different stages
of your lifecycle

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:10.273
depending on how your
organization's implemented.

00:17:10.273 --> 00:17:12.190
One more item I would
like to call out in here

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:14.560
is to also use CI/CD.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:17.920
So as the deployment
process of DAGs

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.780
requires you to place
DAGs into the environment,

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:23.710
you want to make sure that you
control how this flow works.

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:25.210
And there is a lot
of different ways

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.870
you can establish CI/CD pipeline
that leads to a proper DAG

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:29.830
deployment.

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:32.180
We don't have a single solution
as part of our product.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:33.930
But we have recommendations
in our website

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:36.298
that you may want to look
into on how to do it.

00:17:36.298 --> 00:17:37.840
Again, there is not
a single solution

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:39.590
because there is not
a single architecture

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:40.910
that customers follow.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:42.370
But, again I highly
encourage you

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:43.953
to look into the
documentation that we

00:17:43.953 --> 00:17:46.900
have that may help you
establish those CIC pipelines

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:48.880
for your specific use cases.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.480
And one more thing to add
in this specific scenario

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:55.030
is while you are having
your separate environments

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:56.700
for different
stages, please ensure

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.840
you're using the same
versions of Airflow

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:03.190
and also have the same versions
of dependent PyPi modules,

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:06.770
as, otherwise, the testing
may not be reliable enough.

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:09.130
Once you develop
all of your DAGs,

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:12.790
you need to ensure that they
run successfully in production

00:18:12.790 --> 00:18:14.440
and deliver the right results.

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:16.510
And to help in
this specific need,

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:19.420
we have created a
monitoring dashboard

00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:22.460
that is a landing page for
Cloud Composer environments.

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:25.240
And it consists of
different charts and metrics

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:28.390
that you can think of as
three different layers

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.545
of an abstraction in a way.

00:18:30.545 --> 00:18:32.920
So the upper part, which I'm
going to show in the second,

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:36.400
covers statistics and
data around the DAG runs.

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:38.590
The middle part covers
Airflow processes

00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:41.710
and the health of individual
Airflow components.

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:44.380
And the bottom part
covers the infrastructure

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:48.600
and, for instance, metrics
like CPU or memory consumption.

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:50.885
I'm not going to go
through every single chart

00:18:50.885 --> 00:18:52.082
that we're seeing here.

00:18:52.082 --> 00:18:53.540
What's really
important is to learn

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:56.090
how to read those
diagrams and see

00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:58.490
the correlation between
different components

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:00.380
that may occur at the
same point in time.

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:01.880
So, for instance,
imagine that we're

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:04.460
seeing that specific
task started

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:06.080
failing at a point in time.

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:09.520
Then we can look into whether
a DAG bug size have changed.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.560
And, for instance, this failure
that we have just witnessed

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:16.340
may be coming from newly
created and newly deployed DAGs

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:18.890
into our environment,
which actually, if we then

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:23.000
look into Airflow monitoring
or even more so, infrastructure

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.810
monitoring, we can realize that
while these new DAGs have cost

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:29.810
the CPU of the infrastructure
to be maxed out.

00:19:29.810 --> 00:19:31.610
And, therefore,
lack of resources

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:34.400
is probably the reason for
the failure of the tasks.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:38.390
So correlating those different
charts and metrics together

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:41.990
may help you understand the
story behind particular issues

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:44.190
or outages that you
might look into.

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:46.970
Obviously I'm not saying
that the monitoring dashboard

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:48.710
is the only way of
troubleshooting.

00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:51.110
But it can be a
helpful tool in trying

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:53.840
to understand what
might have gone wrong

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:55.530
in some of the DAG executions.

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:58.100
One more recommendation I would
like to pass at this point

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:02.780
is to also establish your
own monitoring alerts

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.310
so that whenever something
goes wrong-- for instance,

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:09.255
you have an unprecedented
error ratio for your DAG runs

00:20:09.255 --> 00:20:10.880
that you're notified
about those cases,

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:14.030
because nobody actually wants
to be looking into those DAG

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:15.480
reports all the time.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.810
We're not currently shipping
any standard set of alerts

00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:21.440
because there is not a single
solution on how to do it

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.030
and how to get notified.

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:24.740
But since all of the
metrics that you're

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:29.030
seeing in this dashboard are
available in the DAG driver,

00:20:29.030 --> 00:20:31.010
you can create alerts
to get notified

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:32.503
about particular challenges.

00:20:32.503 --> 00:20:34.670
And I highly encourage you
to look into that feature

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.640
and use it as well.

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:38.540
Obviously, what's
also very important

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:41.240
when it comes to troubleshooting
your environments

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:42.470
is looking into the logs.

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:44.840
Monitoring is obviously
not everything.

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:47.870
What's important and
where Composer also helps

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:50.000
is by showing you
all of the logs

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.130
from different components
of the infrastructure

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:53.340
in a single place.

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:55.970
So, for instance, as
you can see right now,

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:59.360
I'm currently highlighting
Airflow scheduler logs.

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:01.730
And, therefore, if I want
to look into specific issues

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:05.600
that scheduler may have faced
or may have thrown as errors,

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:07.668
I can easily see it
in a single place

00:21:07.668 --> 00:21:09.960
coming from different components
of the infrastructure.

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:11.522
So that's a handy
tool when it comes

00:21:11.522 --> 00:21:12.980
to troubleshooting
different issues

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:15.500
in different places of
the Composer and Airflow

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:18.032
environment.

00:21:18.032 --> 00:21:19.490
What is also very
important when it

00:21:19.490 --> 00:21:22.490
comes to ensuring that
your DAGs run successfully

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:24.500
is to scale your
environment accordingly.

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:27.110
And there are three components
in the infrastructure that you

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:29.150
can scale independently.

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:31.070
You can scale the Airflow
compute layer, which

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:34.210
is the educated cluster and
add more nodes as your needs

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:35.900
and, therefore,
gets more capacity

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:38.270
for processing your DAG
for running your workers,

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:41.390
for running your tasks
as part of your DAGs.

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:45.530
You can also increase the
size, basically, scale up

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:46.610
and, therefore, database.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.280
If you have DAGs
that are particularly

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:51.500
effort-intensive at
a database layer,

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:53.720
you may choose a larger
database instance

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:55.820
and this way have
additional capacity

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:57.980
at that layer of
the infrastructure.

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:01.220
And the last component you may
want to consider scaling up

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:02.900
is the Airflow web server.

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:05.270
So specifically if you have
a lot of different DAGs

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:07.400
that the web server
has to process, orders

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:09.830
a lot of different users that
want to interact with it,

00:22:09.830 --> 00:22:12.890
you may want to create a
slightly larger web server

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:16.310
to have the Airflow UI
with more capacity as part

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:17.630
of your workload.

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:20.330
However, what is very
important, as well,

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:22.730
when it comes to scaling
of the Airflow web server

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:24.650
is the next feature
I want to introduce.

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:27.290
And that is enable
DAG serialization.

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:28.880
So this is a feature
that is currently

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:32.720
not a default of the
Airflow environment

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:34.160
or if a Composer environment.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:36.110
It may become one at some point.

00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:38.750
But today in the
default approach

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:42.260
to implementation of
Airflow and Composer,

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:45.290
the web server is continuously
processing your DAGs,

00:22:45.290 --> 00:22:47.000
trying to understand
what's inside

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:50.810
and how to visualize them,
and what these DAGs are.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:54.920
If you enable DAG serialization,
however, the Airflow scheduler

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:57.390
is taking one more step,
one more responsibility.

00:22:57.390 --> 00:22:59.330
And that is to
serialize the DAGs

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:01.490
and save the outcome
of that serialization

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:02.780
into the database.

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:04.850
From that point on,
the Airflow web server

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.370
is actually leading the
outcome of that serialization

00:23:07.370 --> 00:23:10.370
instead of going through a
continuous endless loop trying

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:13.120
to run your code as
part of the web server.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:17.090
And this is a significant
reduction of the work

00:23:17.090 --> 00:23:19.160
that the web server
has to perform.

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:22.130
And also as such,
it is significantly

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:25.280
reducing the needs for
capacity of the web server

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:28.220
and the risk of a potential
instability of the web server,

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:32.060
because it no longer needs to
run any somewhat untrusted code

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:33.542
as part of the web server.

00:23:33.542 --> 00:23:35.000
So this is a feature
I would highly

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.340
recommend to reduce the
load of the web server

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:40.110
and increase the stability
of the entire system.

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:43.407
So with that, let me very
quickly recap all the items

00:23:43.407 --> 00:23:44.990
that we have gone
through very quickly

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:49.250
today as a way to
hopefully help you manage

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:53.390
and implement your DAGs in a
way that give you the biggest

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:55.410
benefit from using the product.

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:57.600
So, again, assume that
failures will happen

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:00.380
and are likely to happen even
for the best and the most

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:04.850
reliable end points and to
use built-in, retry, and error

00:24:04.850 --> 00:24:08.150
handling mechanism in Airflow
and, therefore, make your DAG

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:12.380
runs not vulnerable to
those issues and successful

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:13.820
practically all of the time.

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:17.180
To support that also,
make your tasks idempotent

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:19.370
so that they can
be safely retried

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:22.220
without an unexpected
consequences as part

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:23.690
of your DAG executions.

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:25.370
Leave the work to the operators.

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:27.830
Don't try to put the
code in between the tasks

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:30.770
between the operators so
that Airflow can properly

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:32.960
manage the distribution
of the work and errors

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:34.910
that may arise from it.

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:37.040
Use existing Airflow
integrations,

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:40.550
all the operators,
hooks and sensors

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:42.450
that the community has created.

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:44.660
It's a very long and
comprehensive list

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:46.820
and very helpful in getting
you started with most

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:48.380
of the systems around.

00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:50.480
Take time to manage
the Python modules.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:52.660
Ideally without
specifying the versions,

00:24:52.660 --> 00:24:54.740
use the ones that are
available already.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:57.620
Upgrade your environment
as frequently as you can.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:25:00.532
And ideally, don't exceed
the window of six months

00:25:00.532 --> 00:25:02.990
without an upgrade because this
can render your environment

00:25:02.990 --> 00:25:07.250
as outdated and lacking
the recent bug fixes

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:08.630
and improvements.

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:11.040
Use built-in enterprise
security features.

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:13.130
Since this is a long list
of potential features,

00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:14.630
you may need to
choose the ones that

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:17.750
fit your organization and
your security policies

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:19.580
and regulations.

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:23.000
But look into those, please,
and choose ones accordingly.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:28.010
For managing of your secrets
and keys with other systems,

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:31.160
highly recommend to use
Secret Manager as a way

00:25:31.160 --> 00:25:34.250
to manage basically
all of those components

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:36.790
that you need to have
for your integrations.

00:25:36.790 --> 00:25:39.020
Use separate for the known
for the environments,

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:41.240
use CI/CD, and
ensure you're running

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:43.220
the same versions in
all the environments

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:44.960
with the same packages.

00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:48.360
Use Composers built in
monitoring dashboards and logs.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.860
And if possible, also establish
and set up your own outlets

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:54.650
so that you get notified on any
potential outages and issues

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:55.810
that you may encounter.

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:57.500
Scale your environment
accordingly,

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.000
remembering that these are three
different elements that you

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.510
can scale independently-- the
compute layer, the database

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:04.170
layer, and the web server.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:06.410
And then use the
DAG serialization

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:08.690
to reduce the load
of the web server

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:11.570
and increase the stability
of the web server

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:14.030
as a component of
the overall platform.

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:16.950
With that, I wanted to thank
you very much for attention.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.650
I hope some of those
hints where useful.

00:26:19.650 --> 00:26:22.800
And I'm looking forward to
see you using Cloud Composer

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:25.900
and contributing also to
Apache Airflow community.

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:27.710
Thank you very much.

